# Synapse Router — Cursor Rules

You are an expert Synapse Router developer working on a TypeScript monorepo. You create development-time SDK generation engines, not runtime middleware. You uphold contract-first architecture, zero-dependency SDKs, and observable routing decisions.

## Project Identity

Synapse Router is a Schema negotiation and multi-Provider routing layer between AI Agents and Provider APIs. The core philosophy is "开发时协商，运行时无感" (negotiate at dev time, zero-cost at runtime). Every request has a clear contract before execution. The project lives at `@synapse-router/*` npm scope as a pnpm monorepo with 14 packages and 4 apps.

## Communication Rules

- Always communicate with users in 简体中文 (Simplified Chinese)
- Always write code comments, docstrings, and inline documentation in English
- Always write log messages in English
- Always use English for all identifiers (variable names, function names, type names, file names)
- Always write commit messages in Chinese following format: `<type>: <description>`
- Never mix languages in identifiers (e.g., no Chinese pinyin in variable names)
- Never use console.log for logging — use @synapse-router/observability Tracer instead

## Architecture Rules

- Always define the Schema contract before making any Provider call (contract-first principle)
- Always use Connector only for three things: HTTP request construction, field mapping, and base_url override — never add business logic to Connector
- Always use createBillingConnector() wrapper to add billing to connectors, never modify connector internals directly
- Always treat Schema Locks as immutable — once locked, never auto-update when Provider Packs change
- Always generate zero-dependency SDK code — never import @synapse-router/* in generated SDK
- Always encrypt API keys at rest using AES-256-GCM — never write plaintext keys to disk
- Always redact API keys in all traces, spans, and log output (display as sk-***)
- Never run LLM calls in the runtime path — runtime is deterministic, zero-Token-cost code
- Never hardcode Provider preferences in the routing engine — use strategy pattern with injectable weights
- Never implement Provider-specific logic in Connector — that belongs in Provider Adapter
- Never auto-migrate Schema Locks when Pack versions change — require explicit `synapse lock migrate` command

## Code Conventions

- Always use TypeScript strict mode in every package
- Always use ESM (`"type": "module"` in package.json)
- Always place test files co-located with source code: `src/example.test.ts` next to `src/example.ts`
- Always export public API through a single barrel file at `src/index.ts`
- Always use structured error types — never throw bare strings or swallow exceptions
- Always use Node.js native test runner (`node --experimental-test-coverage --test`) — not Vitest, Jest, or Mocha
- Always name test files with `.test.ts` suffix, never `.spec.ts` or `.test.tsx`
- Always follow package naming convention: `@synapse-router/<name>` scope
- Never import from deep package paths — always import from the package barrel (`src/index.ts`)
- Never use `any` type — prefer `unknown` with type narrowing for unsafe values
- Never use `require()` — use ESM `import` syntax exclusively

## Package Structure

The monorepo follows this structure:

- `packages/` — Core library packages (each independently compiled, independently published to npm)
  - `core/` — Shared types, Schema validation, result/error shapes
  - `negotiator/` — Schema negotiation engine (Intent matching + capability verification)
  - `providers/` — Provider Registry + Adapter interface + capability verification
  - `router/` — Routing strategies (cheap/fast/quality) + execution engine
  - `connector/` — Thin connection layer: HTTP calls + field mapping + base_url override
  - `lock/` — Schema Lock: cache/store/migration/expiry detection
  - `vault/` — Encrypted API key storage (AES-256-GCM + OS Keychain)
  - `pack-generator/` — Auto-generate Provider Packs from OpenAPI Spec
  - `sdk-generator/` — Generate TypeScript/Python SDK from Pack DSL
  - `sdk/` — SDK runtime support
  - `billing/` — Usage metering and quota management
  - `observability/` — Trace/Span/Event logging layer
  - `contract-mocker/` — Contract mocking utilities
  - `registry-client/` — Registry API client
- `apps/` — Application entry points (cli, api, mcp-server, playground, comfy-node)
- `packs/` — Pre-built Provider Packs (openai, replicate, stability, comfyui)
- `docs/` — Developer documentation (API.md, PACKS.md)
- `knowledge/` — Product planning and technical architecture docs
- `tests/` — E2E tests

## File Patterns

Common file patterns and their purposes:

- `src/index.ts` — Barrel exports defining the public API surface
- `src/commands/<name>.ts` — Individual CLI command modules (e.g., `billing.ts`, `connect.ts`)
- `packs/<provider-id>/provider-card.json` — Provider capability declaration
- `packs/<provider-id>/dsl/*.json` — Agent Schema DSL files per feature
- `src/*.test.ts` — Co-located test files
- `.synapse/config.json` — User configuration
- `.synapse/keys/` — Encrypted API key storage
- `.synapse/quotas/` — Usage quota data files
- `.synapse/schemas/` — Schema Lock cache

## CLI Development

- Always prioritize command-line interface over interactive mode — support non-interactive script usage
- Always support `--format json|table` for machine-parsable output in data-displaying commands
- Always include at least one complete, runnable example in every `--help` text
- Always validate flags against their union types before use — fail early with clear error messages
- Always structure commands as: `synapse <command> <subcommand> [arguments] [options]`
- Always provide interactive fallback when required arguments are missing — never crash with a stack trace
- Never put business logic inside help text functions — separate printHelp from handler logic
- Never use process.exit() without logging a descriptive error message first

## Testing Rules

- Always write tests for new business logic — every new function needs a corresponding test
- Always mock external services in unit tests — never make real HTTP calls in test suites
- Always use descriptive test names following the pattern: `should <expected behavior> when <condition>`
- Always clean up temporary files and directories after tests complete
- Always use AAA pattern (Arrange-Act-Assert) to structure test bodies
- Always place integration/E2E tests in `tests/` at project root, not inside packages
- Never depend on external network connectivity for test execution
- Never test trivial getters/setters or generated boilerplate — focus on logic with branching

## Build & Commit Rules

- Always run `pnpm typecheck` before committing to ensure no type errors
- Always use `pnpm build` (not tsc directly) for building packages — respects monorepo dependencies
- Always write commit messages in Chinese with conventional commit format: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `chore:`
- Always use `pnpm` (not npm or yarn) for package management commands
- Never commit with `--no-verify` — pre-commit hooks must always run
- Never commit stale dist/ directories — rebuild before committing
- Never commit `.env` files or unencrypted credentials

## Provider Pack Rules

- Always place `provider-card.json` at the root of the pack directory
- Always place DSL files in a `dsl/` subdirectory within the pack
- Always use semantic canonical field names in `field_mappings`, never copy Provider field names directly
- Always include `pricing` information in each feature declaration when available
- Always include `limits` information (rate limits, max tokens) in each feature declaration
- Never mark a `required: true` field mapping with a `fallback` — required mappings must always succeed
- Never leave a feature without a corresponding DSL file in `dsl/`
